# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sanity content system
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values from your own
# Sanity project. Until NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_PROJECT_ID is set, every page
# keeps rendering the content in src/data/ exactly as it does today —
# see isSanityConfigured in src/sanity/env.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# From sanity.io/manage → your project → Project ID.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_PROJECT_ID=

# The dataset name. "production" unless you deliberately made another.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_DATASET=production

# Pins the API contract. Do not bump without re-testing the queries.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_API_VERSION=2025-02-19

# Where the embedded Studio is mounted.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_STUDIO_URL=/studio

# The origin the Presentation tool loads for previews. Match the dev port.
SANITY_STUDIO_PREVIEW_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# A **Viewer** token from Sanity Manage → API → Tokens.
# Server-only: never give this the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
SANITY_API_READ_TOKEN=

# Any long random string. Sent by the publish webhook as
# `Authorization: Bearer <this>` and checked in
# src/app/api/revalidate/sanity/route.ts.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
SANITY_REVALIDATE_SECRET=

# An **Editor** token, needed only if you script content imports.
# Leave empty for normal running — nothing the site serves reads it.
# Used by `npm run sanity:migrate` and by `npm run sanity:images`,
# which uploads the photographs in public/uploads/ into the CMS so an
# editor can swap one without a deploy. Run `npm run sanity:images:dry`
# first: it writes nothing and prints exactly what would change.
SANITY_API_WRITE_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Instagram feed  (src/components/property/InstagramFeedGrid.tsx)
#
# One workspace feed URL per ACCOUNT, and there are three of them:
# @nyuhbalivillas (Seminyak), @nyuhbaliubud (Ubud) and @mahamayaspa.ubud
# (the SPA band on /ubud/spa). Pointing two of these at one workspace
# publishes the same posts under two different names — check the
# `profile.username` a URL returns before trusting it.
#
# Server-side only: the browser calls /api/instagram/<feed>, which proxies
# this. The feed service sends no CORS headers, so a direct call from the
# page is blocked — and the proxy keeps this URL private.
# Leave empty to fall back to the hand-picked stills.
#
# Sanity wins over these when the property document sets it:
# instagramApiUrl (the resort) and spaInstagramApiUrl (Ubud only, the spa).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
INSTAGRAM_FEED_SEMINYAK=
INSTAGRAM_FEED_UBUD=
INSTAGRAM_FEED_SPA=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare Turnstile  (the bot check on every form)
#
# Both keys come from one widget: dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile →
# Add widget. Add every hostname the site serves from to that widget,
# localhost included, or the challenge fails with a hostname error.
#
# Leave BOTH empty and the forms behave exactly as they did before
# Turnstile existed: no widget renders and nothing is verified. Setting
# only one is the one broken state — the site key alone shows a widget
# whose token /api/contact will always refuse.
#
# The site key is inlined at build time (NEXT_PUBLIC_), so changing it
# needs a rebuild, not just a restart. The secret is read per request in
# src/server/turnstile.ts and must never gain the prefix.
#
# Cloudflare publishes fixed test keys, useful for exercising each path
# locally without touching the real widget's analytics:
#   always passes  site 1x00000000000000000000AA
#                  secret 1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#   always blocks  site 2x00000000000000000000AB
#                  secret 2x0000000000000000000000000000000AA
#   forces the interactive challenge  site 3x00000000000000000000FF
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SendGrid  (what every form actually sends through)
#
# Handled by src/server/sendgrid.ts, over SendGrid's v3 REST API —
# there is no @sendgrid/mail dependency to install.
#
# SENDGRID_API_KEY
#   app.sendgrid.com → Settings → API Keys → Create API Key. "Restricted
#   Access" with **Mail Send** alone is enough; nothing here reads
#   templates, contacts or statistics. The key is shown once. Server-only:
#   never give it the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
#   **One key, however many domains.** A key belongs to the SendGrid
#   account, not to a sender or a domain, so the same key sends from
#   every identity that account has verified. Two keys would only be
#   needed for two separate SendGrid accounts, which this does not
#   support — say so if that is ever the setup.
#
#   It is read per request, so rotating it takes effect on the next
#   submission rather than the next deploy. Whitespace around it is
#   trimmed, and a value that is not shaped `SG.<id>.<secret>` is named
#   in the log — pasting the key's *name* instead of the key otherwise
#   shows up only as SendGrid's opaque 401.
#
#   Leave it empty and the forms still work end to end — the bot check
#   runs, the visitor gets their confirmation — but nothing is delivered,
#   and the server log says so on every submission. That is the state a
#   preview deploy runs in; it is not a state to launch in.
#
# ── WHO THE MAIL IS SENT *AS* ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The single most common thing to get wrong: SendGrid refuses (403,
# "does not match a verified Sender Identity") anything sent from an
# address it has not verified. Verify under Settings → Sender
# Authentication — a whole domain if you can edit its DNS, otherwise a
# Single Sender per address.
#
# It must be an address at a domain you control, NOT the visitor's own.
# Sending as the visitor fails SPF/DKIM and lands in spam; their address
# goes in Reply-To instead, which the route fills in automatically from
# the form's own email field.
#
# SENDGRID_FROM_SEMINYAK / SENDGRID_FROM_UBUD
#   One sender per property, for the two domains this business runs.
#   Use these when BOTH domains are authenticated in SendGrid, so each
#   resort's mail leaves from its own domain.
#
# SENDGRID_FROM_EMAIL
#   The fallback used for any property without its own sender above.
#   If only one domain is authenticated, set just this one and leave the
#   per-property pair empty.
#
# SENDGRID_FROM_NAME
#   Optional display name for both. Unset, it is "Nyuh Bali Seminyak" /
#   "Nyuh Bali Ubud".
#
# ── WHERE THE MAIL LANDS ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CONTACT_TO_SEMINYAK / CONTACT_TO_UBUD
#   Optional — unset, each property's published address in
#   src/data/properties.ts is used (reservation@nyuhbalivillas.com and
#   info@ubudnyuhbali.com, already two different domains). These need no
#   verification of any kind; only senders do. Set them on a staging
#   deploy so test submissions reach you and not the resort.
#
# CONTACT_CC
#   Comma-separated extra recipients, copied on every enquiry from both
#   properties. Meant for the period when someone needs to watch real
#   submissions arrive **without** diverting them away from the resort;
#   remove it afterwards, because the resort sees these addresses in the
#   message header. An entry equal to that property's own recipient is
#   dropped automatically — SendGrid rejects the entire send if one
#   address appears twice across to/cc/bcc.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SENDGRID_API_KEY=
SENDGRID_FROM_SEMINYAK=
SENDGRID_FROM_UBUD=
SENDGRID_FROM_EMAIL=
SENDGRID_FROM_NAME=
CONTACT_TO_SEMINYAK=
CONTACT_TO_UBUD=
CONTACT_CC=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare cache  (auto-purge on publish + the zone's cache rules)
#
# Only needed once the domain is served through Cloudflare. Until then
# leave all of this empty: the publish webhook still revalidates Next's
# own cache exactly as it always has, and the purge step reports
# "not-configured" instead of failing. That is the state a preview
# deployment runs in.
#
# Full walkthrough — DNS, the cache rules, and how to verify a purge —
# in README-CLOUDFLARE.md.
#
# Check the pair with `npm run cache:check`, which verifies the token
# and reports what the edge currently holds without purging anything.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID
#   dash.cloudflare.com → the domain → Overview → right-hand sidebar,
#   "Zone ID". Not a secret, but not useful to anyone either.
#
#   ⚠ It sits next to the ACCOUNT ID and both are 32 hex characters.
#   Pasting the wrong one is the single most common failure here;
#   `npm run cache:check` catches it before a purge does.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_PURGE_TOKEN
#   My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token → Custom token.
#     Permissions:    Zone → Cache Purge → Purge
#     Zone Resources: Include → Specific zone → this one
#
#   ⚠ Give it that ONE permission. A token that can only empty a cache
#   is worthless if it leaks; an "Edit zone" token can repoint the
#   domain at another server. This is the only Cloudflare credential
#   that belongs in the deployment's environment.
#
# SITE_URL
#   The public origin Cloudflare knows the site by, e.g.
#   https://nyuhbalivillas.com. Optional: the CLI uses it to show what
#   the edge is serving before and after a purge, and URL-mode purges
#   need it to build absolute URLs. Not VERCEL_URL — the *.vercel.app
#   host is not proxied, so nothing of it is cached.
#
#   ⚠ It is read outside Cloudflare too. SITE_ORIGIN in src/data/seo.ts
#   takes it as the origin for every canonical link, every og:url, the
#   sitemap's <loc> entries and robots.txt's Sitemap: line. Unset, all of
#   those fall back to https://nyuhbalivillas.com rather than to the
#   deployment's own host — deliberately, so a preview deployment points
#   its canonicals at the live site instead of nominating itself as the
#   canonical copy. Set it only to a domain that should own those URLs.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_PURGE_MODE
#   "everything" (default) or "urls". Everything is one API call and is
#   always complete; "urls" purges only the pages a published document
#   affects, which is faster on a busy zone but misses copies cached
#   under a query string (/ubud/villa?utm_source=ig is a separate entry
#   from /ubud/villa). Leave it unset unless you have read the caveat in
#   README-CLOUDFLARE.md.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_RULES_TOKEN
#   A SECOND, WIDER token — Zone → Cache Rules → Edit — used by
#   `npm run cloudflare:rules` and by nothing at runtime. Keep it in
#   your own .env.local; it does not belong in Vercel or in CI.
#
# ENFORCE_CANONICAL_HOST
#   "1" to make a PRODUCTION deployment 308 its own *.vercel.app alias to
#   SITE_URL. SET THIS AT CUTOVER, once DNS points at Vercel.
#
#   Unset (the default), that alias is served normally and answers
#   X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow instead. That is deliberate for the
#   window this project is in: nyuhbalivillas.com still resolves to
#   WordPress, so the alias is the only address the finished build can be
#   looked at, and a 308 would send the reviewer to the very site the
#   build replaces.
#
#   Both modes keep the alias out of the index — noindex is a directive,
#   not the hint a canonical tag is. The 308 additionally consolidates
#   ranking signals, which is worth having once the alias duplicates a
#   live site and is worth nothing before that, because nothing links to
#   a *.vercel.app address.
#
#   So forgetting either value is not a duplicate-content incident, and
#   nothing here can reach a visitor on nyuhbalivillas.com: real traffic
#   arrives at the canonical host, where the proxy does not act at all.
#
#   It changes nothing on localhost or on preview deployments; neither is
#   touched in the first place.
#
# Server-only: never give any of these the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_PURGE_TOKEN=
SITE_URL=
CLOUDFLARE_PURGE_MODE=
CLOUDFLARE_RULES_TOKEN=
ENFORCE_CANONICAL_HOST=
